  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1141 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nToday - Friday: Cooler, Periodic Sprinkles/Flurries \n\nCooler temperatures continue to filter into the area as colder \nair through the lower and mid troposphere sags southward behind \nthe departing trough axis and surface low. With this cold air \nadvection expected to linger to some degree over the coming \ndays, high temperatures are expected to struggle to climb into \nthe lower 40s both today and Friday. \n\nWeak upper level impulses will pass overhead through the broadly \ncyclonic flow aloft. As this interacts with a fetch of moisture off \nof Lake Superior, steep low level lapse rates and low amounts of \ninstability look to be enough to support periodic sprinkles or even \nflurries, even amidst the lack of moisture below 800 mb. Little\nto no impacts are anticipated from these very light showers \nwith any precipitation likely coming to an end for most by this \nevening. Locations in the Chimney/North Central Wisconsin could \nsee flurries/sprinkles continue into Friday morning owing to \ncloser proximity to the Lake Superior moisture source. \n\nThis Weekend into Mid Next Week: Dry and Slowly Warming \n\nAn omega block sets up aloft over the High Plains/Rockies by \nSaturday with a strong Canadian high pressure dropping south into \nNorthern Plains/Upper Midwest. This blocking pattern, which \nredevelops into a broad upper ridge by early next week, and the \nstrengthening surface high will dominate our pattern with little to \nno precipitation expected between Friday and next Wednesday. Modest \nreturn flow by the start of the work week will allow for a gradual \nwarm up back into the 50s by mid week. The next chance of \nprecipitation doesn't look to come until next Thursday when the \nupper ridge finally breaks down, lending to the potential \nfor a shortwave trough to move through. \n\nAn amplification of the upper level pattern for the end of next week \ndoes set the stage for a potentially more active stretch of weather \nleading into the Thanksgiving holiday. How this pattern evolves \nremains to be seen, but impactful snow somewhere in the Upper \nMidwest is one plausible scenario that is showing up in a few \nensemble members.",
